  "worked_solution": "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt{85}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose squares bracket $85$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt{85}$ lies.\nWe know that ${9}^{2}=81$ and ${10}^{2}=100$.\nSince $81<85<100$, we know that $9<\\sqrt{85}<10$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $9$ and $10$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9.1}^{2} & =82.81 \\\\ {9.2}^{2} & =84.64 \\\\ {9.3}^{2} & =86.49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $84.64<85<86.49$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{9.2}^{2}<85<{9.3}^{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n9.2<\\sqrt{85}<9.3\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt{85}$ lies between $9.2$ and $9.3$.",
